  <section class="bk-intro" aria-labelledby="bk-intro-h">
    <h2 id="bk-intro-h">Are parliaments really opening their doors to citizens?</h2>
    <p>Trust in political institutions has worn thin, and parliaments sit at the centre of that problem. <em>Open Parliaments</em> asks whether they can help repair it: do they keep their promises of openness and public engagement, or is the change mostly symbolic?</p>
    <p>The book compares twenty-one European parliaments and follows two in-depth cases, Austria and France, where new engagement practices have become part of parliamentary routine. It finds a narrow openness. Information is published and participation is possible, yet material is hard to read, participatory tools rarely carry consequences, and those who take part are mostly people already close to power.</p>
  </section>

